You are selecting one constrained repair for a tiny synthetic web menu. The mouse works, but keyboard users cannot open it. Preserve mouse behavior, make Tab/Enter/Space work, and use correct native HTML semantics. Return exactly JSON with keys schema (repair-bench.a11y-proposal.v1) and repair (one of noop, aria_only, native_button). Do not add fields, markdown, or code. Here is the original source:
<!doctype html>
<!-- Repair Bench synthetic accessibility fixture. GPT-6, 2026-10-28. -->
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>Repair Bench — Keyboard Menu Fixture</title>
<style>
body { font: 18px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif; max-width: 44rem; margin: 3rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; }
#open-menu { display: inline-block; border: 2px solid #333; border-radius: .5rem; padding: .6rem 1rem; background: #f3f4f6; color: #111; font: inherit; cursor: pointer; }
#open-menu:focus-visible, #close-menu:focus-visible { outline: 4px solid #005fcc; outline-offset: 3px; }
#panel { border: 2px solid #777; padding: 1rem; margin-top: 1rem; border-radius: .5rem; }
[hidden] { display: none !important; }
</style></head><body>
<h1>Repair Bench: local menu fixture</h1>
<p>Open the sample menu using only a keyboard. A mouse must continue to work.</p>
<div id="open-menu" data-repair-target="menu-trigger" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="panel">Open menu</div>
<section id="panel" hidden aria-label="Menu panel"><p>Menu content</p><button id="close-menu" type="button">Close menu</button></section>
<script>
(() => {
  const trigger = document.getElementById('open-menu');
  const panel = document.getElementById('panel');
  function setOpen(open) {
    panel.hidden = !open;
    trigger.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(open));
    if (!open) trigger.focus();
  }
  trigger.addEventListener('click', () => setOpen(true));
  document.getElementById('close-menu').addEventListener('click', () => setOpen(false));
})();
</script></body></html>
